    "q-89355": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89355",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\nI. The number of boys in a family of $3$ children\n\nII. The number of red cars passing through an avenue in an hour\n\nIII. The number of pizzas sold by a pizzeria in a day",
      "worked_solution": "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of boys can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although being red can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of pizzas sold cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
